U('storage-choice',2,'本地保存与云端同步的取舍','方案分析',[
 ['A team is building a personal writing tool and must decide whether to store drafts only on the current device or synchronise them through a cloud service. The choice is often presented as a simple contest between privacy and convenience. In practice, the decision involves several separate questions: who controls the data, how a person recovers from a lost device, what happens without a network connection, and how conflicting edits are resolved. A useful proposal should make these questions visible instead of hiding them behind a label such as “secure” or “modern”. Neither label tells a user what the product actually does.','一个团队正在开发个人写作工具，需要决定草稿只保存在当前设备，还是通过云服务同步。这个选择常被简化为隐私与便利之间的竞争，实际上却包含多个问题：谁控制数据、设备丢失后如何恢复、断网时如何运行，以及如何处理冲突修改。好的提案应明确这些问题，而不是藏在“安全”“现代”等标签背后，因为这些词并不能说明产品实际做了什么。'],
 ['Local storage allows the basic writing experience to work without an account or a network connection. However, it does not by itself provide a backup. If the device fails or the user clears the relevant browser data, locally saved drafts may be lost. The interface therefore needs a clear export option and an explanation of what is included. A backup that cannot be restored is of limited value, so the team should test the import process as well. It should also avoid describing local storage as completely private: other people using the same browser profile may still be able to read the drafts.','本地保存使基础写作功能无需账号或网络也能使用，但它本身并不提供备份。设备损坏或用户清理相应浏览器数据时，草稿可能丢失。因此界面需要清晰的导出入口，并说明备份包含什么。无法恢复的备份价值有限，团队也应测试导入流程。另外，不应将本地保存描述为绝对私密：使用同一浏览器配置的其他人仍可能读到草稿。'],
 ['Cloud synchronisation makes moving between devices easier, but introduces account management, service availability, and conflict handling. A successful upload is not the same thing as a complete recovery strategy. If a user accidentally deletes a paragraph and that deletion immediately reaches every device, synchronisation has worked exactly as designed while the user has still lost useful work. Version history or a separate backup may be needed. Similarly, a cloud icon should distinguish pending changes from confirmed uploads. When the service is unavailable, users should know whether they can continue writing and what will happen to their unsent changes.','云同步方便用户跨设备使用，但引入了账号管理、服务可用性和冲突处理。成功上传不等于完整的恢复策略：如果用户误删一段内容，而删除操作立即同步到所有设备，同步虽然按设计工作，用户却仍丢失了有用内容，因此可能还需要版本历史或单独备份。同样，云图标应区分待同步修改与已确认上传。服务不可用时，用户应知道能否继续写，以及未发送修改会如何处理。'],
 ['For this team, a reasonable first release could use local storage with tested export and import, while keeping cloud synchronisation optional for a later release. That is a decision about present priorities, not a claim that local storage is always superior. The proposal should name the assumptions behind it: early users work mainly on one device, the team has limited time to operate an online service, and losing a draft would be costly. If those assumptions change, the team should reconsider the choice. A decision record is most useful when it explains when the recommendation would stop being appropriate, not merely why it seemed attractive at the time.','对这个团队而言，合理的首版方案可以是本地保存加经过测试的导入导出，之后再提供可选云同步。这是对当前优先级的判断，并不意味着本地保存永远更好。提案应说明其假设：早期用户主要使用一台设备、团队运营在线服务的时间有限，而丢失草稿的代价较高。假设改变时应重新评估。决策记录除了说明当时为何有吸引力，还应说明什么情况下建议不再合适。']
],[['by itself','单凭自身','Local storage does not by itself provide a backup.'],['of limited value','价值有限','A backup without recovery is of limited value.'],['as designed','按设计','The system worked as designed.'],['rather than','而不是','Explain the behaviour rather than adding a label.'],['reconsider the choice','重新考虑选择','Reconsider the choice if the assumptions change.']],[[
 '为什么作者说同步成功仍可能丢失内容？',['云服务永远不保存数据','误删也可能同步到所有设备','所有本地文件都会被关闭'],1,'第三段以同步误删为例，区分同步和可恢复的历史备份。'],
 ['作者推荐的首版方案依赖什么？',['本地保存永远优于云端','所有用户拒绝账号','当前用户习惯、团队资源和丢稿代价等假设'],2,'最后一段强调建议依赖当前优先级与明确假设，不能无限推广。'],
 ['一份有用的决策记录还应写什么？',['建议何时不再适用','更多笼统标签','保证永不改变方案'],0,'结尾强调说明推荐失效的条件，而不只列当初的优点。']
 ],{task:'为个人学习网站写一份英文存储方案建议，比较本地保存和可选云同步。',audience:'一起开发产品的同事',length:'200～280 词',requirements:['给出建议及适用的用户场景','比较备份、断网与跨设备使用','说明至少两个假设和重新评估的条件'],starter:'For the first release, I recommend…\nThis recommendation assumes…\nWe should reconsider it if…',model:'For the first release, I recommend local storage with reliable export and import. This keeps the basic learning workflow available without an account or a network connection. It also reduces the amount of online service work required from a small team. However, local storage is not a backup. Users need an obvious way to export their records, and we should test whether the exported file can restore drafts and review history.\n\nCloud synchronisation would help people who regularly switch devices. It would also require us to explain upload status, handle conflicting edits, and provide a recovery strategy for accidental deletion. We should not treat a successful upload as proof that every past version can be recovered.\n\nThis recommendation assumes that most early users work on one device and that the team cannot yet maintain an account service reliably. We should reconsider it if cross-device use becomes a common need or if local-only records create frequent support problems. A later cloud option should preserve offline writing and make its limitations clear.\n\nFor the initial trial, we should export a record containing drafts, vocabulary and review notes, clear the test account, and restore the file. A visible export button is not enough unless the restored result contains the information users expected to keep.'});
U('proposal',2,'提出改进，而不是只说“更好”','工作提案',[
 ['Our team currently answers installation questions through individual messages. This feels personal, but it creates repeated work and makes it difficult for new users to find answers that already exist. I propose a short troubleshooting guide organised around observable symptoms. The goal is not to replace personal support. It is to help users solve common problems independently and give the support team a consistent starting point when a problem needs further investigation. A useful guide should explain what to check, what a result means, and what information to send if the suggested steps do not help.','我们目前通过私人消息回答安装问题。这很有人情味，却造成重复劳动，新用户也难以找到已有答案。我建议制作一份按可观察症状组织的简短排障指南。目标并非取代人工支持，而是帮助用户独立解决常见问题，并为需要进一步调查的情况提供一致起点。指南应说明要检查什么、检查结果意味着什么，以及步骤无效时应提交哪些信息。'],
 ['The first version should cover only the three issues that appear most often in recent support conversations. Each section would begin with the exact message a user might see, followed by a small number of safe checks. Instructions should avoid assuming that readers know internal component names. For example, “open the connection settings” is more useful than “verify the transport configuration” unless the screen actually uses the latter wording. Screenshots may help users find a control, but they should not carry the only copy of an important instruction. Written steps are easier to search, update, and use with assistive technology.','首版只覆盖近期支持对话中最常见的三个问题。每节先展示用户可能看到的准确消息，再列出少量安全检查。说明不应假定读者知道内部组件名称。例如，除非界面实际使用相应术语，“打开连接设置”比“验证传输配置”更容易理解。截图能帮助定位控件，但重要说明不应只存在于截图中；文字步骤更便于搜索、更新和通过辅助技术使用。'],
 ['I suggest asking two people who did not write the guide to try it with a sample installation. We should observe where they pause or choose an unexpected action, rather than immediately explaining the intended meaning. Their behaviour can reveal assumptions that the authors no longer notice. This small test will not prove that the guide works for everyone, but it can identify avoidable confusion before publication. We should also ask the support team whether the guide requests enough diagnostic information. A clear article that leaves out the relevant error log may still fail to reduce the number of messages needed to solve a case.','我建议请两位没有参与编写的人用示例安装来试读。观察他们在哪里停顿或选择意外操作，不要立即解释原意。他们的行为能暴露作者已察觉不到的假设。这种小测试无法证明指南对所有人都有效，但能在发布前找出可避免的困惑。还应询问支持团队，指南是否要求提供足够诊断信息。即使文章清楚，若漏掉重要日志，也仍可能无法减少解决问题所需的消息数量。'],
 ['After publication, we can review a small set of measures: how often the guide is shared, whether users complete the suggested checks, and whether repeated questions become less common. Page views alone will not establish success; a frequently opened page might be confusing rather than helpful. We should keep a visible route to contact support, record unanswered questions, and update the guide when the interface changes. I am asking for agreement on this limited first version, not approval for a large documentation project. If it does not help, we can revise the approach before investing in more articles.','发布后，可以观察指南被分享的频率、用户是否完成建议检查，以及重复问题是否减少。浏览量本身不能证明成功：高频打开也可能意味着页面令人困惑。应保留明显的支持入口，记录未回答的问题，并随界面变化更新指南。我请求同意的是这个范围有限的首版，而非大型文档项目；若它无效，可以先调整方法，再投入更多文章。']
],[['observable symptoms','可观察的症状','Organise the guide around observable symptoms.'],['starting point','起点','Use the guide as a consistent starting point.'],['leave out','漏掉','Do not leave out the relevant error log.'],['establish success','证明成功','Page views alone do not establish success.'],['limited first version','范围有限的首版','Agree on a limited first version.']],[[
 '作者希望指南取代人工支持吗？',['希望彻底取消支持','没有，指南为常见问题提供帮助并支持后续调查','只允许老用户寻求帮助'],1,'第一段明确说目标不是取代 personal support。'],
 ['为什么试读时不应立刻解释原意？',['为了故意让人失败','因为作者不懂内容','为了观察并发现说明中的隐藏假设'],2,'第三段强调观察停顿和意外操作能揭示作者忽略的假设。'],
 ['高浏览量能单独证明指南成功吗？',['不能，也可能是读者困惑而反复打开','能，越多越好','能，说明所有问题都已解决'],0,'第四段明确反对仅用 page views 建立成功结论。']
 ],{task:'用英文提议一项范围有限的工作改进，例如增加安装指南、调整设置页或改善错误提示。',audience:'决定是否投入时间的团队负责人',length:'200～280 词',requirements:['说明具体问题与受影响的人','界定首版范围并提出验证方式','说明风险、成本或方法失效的可能'],starter:'The current process…\nI propose a first version that…\nWe can evaluate it by…',model:'Users currently ask the same installation questions in separate messages. I propose a short guide covering the three most common problems from recent support conversations. Each section should show the message users see, explain a few checks, and list the information needed if those checks do not solve the problem. The guide should keep a clear route to personal support.\n\nBefore publication, two people who did not write it should try the instructions on a sample installation. We should record where they hesitate instead of explaining each step immediately. A support teammate should also confirm that we request useful logs without asking for unrelated information.\n\nAfter release, we can review whether repeated questions become less common and whether users can complete the checks. Page views will provide context, but they will not be our only measure. The main cost is keeping instructions aligned with the interface. I suggest starting with this limited version and reviewing its usefulness before adding more articles.\n\nIf readers still need several explanations for the same step, we should revise that section before expanding the guide. We should also record who owns each article and when it was last checked. Otherwise, a growing collection could create outdated instructions instead of reducing confusion.'});
U('ai-learning',2,'AI 能帮你检查，但不能替你理解','观点文章',[
 ['An English learner can use an AI assistant to make difficult material easier to approach. It can explain an unfamiliar phrase, show the structure of a sentence, or suggest a clearer version of a draft. These are useful forms of support, especially for someone who reads technical documents without a teacher nearby. However, receiving a clear explanation is not the same as being able to understand the original text independently. A productive learning routine needs moments when the learner works without immediate assistance and then checks what they understood. Otherwise, the apparent fluency of the tool can hide the uncertainty of the person using it.','英语学习者可以用 AI 降低困难材料的门槛：解释陌生短语、分析句子结构，或为初稿提出更清楚的说法。对独自阅读技术文档的人，这些帮助很有价值。但收到清楚解释，不等于能独立理解原文。有效的练习需要先独立尝试，再核对理解；否则工具的流畅可能掩盖使用者的不确定。'],
 ['Consider a learner who pastes every paragraph into a translator before reading it. The person may finish more pages, yet have few opportunities to connect English expressions directly with meaning. A different routine is to read a manageable passage first, write a short account of its main point, and ask the assistant to identify important misunderstandings. The learner can then return to the passage and see whether it now makes sense without the explanation. This approach still uses assistance, but places the learner in the role of making and revising an interpretation rather than simply consuming a finished translation. The passage should be challenging enough to teach something without making independent reading impossible.','设想有人每读一段前都先粘贴翻译。他可能完成更多页数，却很少有机会直接建立英文表达与含义之间的联系。另一种方式是先读难度适当的段落，简述主旨，再让助手指出重要误解，然后回到原文检查能否独立读懂。这依然使用帮助，但学习者在主动形成和修正理解，而非只消费成品译文。材料应有挑战，却不应难到无法独立阅读。'],
 ['Writing presents a related problem. A polished replacement may remove errors while also removing the learner’s decisions. If the assistant changes the intended meaning, introduces facts that were not supplied, or makes a simple message unnecessarily formal, the final text may look impressive without serving its purpose. Feedback is more useful when it separates genuine errors from optional style changes and explains why a revision helps. The learner should keep the first draft, choose which suggestions to accept, and write a revised version. A short note explaining one important change can provide stronger evidence of learning than a high score with no explanation.','写作也有类似问题。一篇润色好的替代文本可能消除了错误，也消除了学习者自己的决策。如果助手改变原意、加入未提供的事实，或把简单消息改得过分正式，成品可能显得漂亮，却无法完成目的。反馈若能区分真正错误与可选风格优化，并解释修改理由，就更有价值。学习者应保留初稿、选择建议、亲自重写。解释一个重要修改，可能比没有理由的高分更能体现学习。'],
 ['This does not mean that every sentence requires a detailed grammar lesson or that every suggestion must be challenged. The amount of attention should match the task. A routine message may need only a quick check, while a proposal affecting other people deserves more careful revision. Over time, the learner can look for recurring problems, practise them deliberately, and check whether the same support is needed less often. Success is not measured by using the assistant as little as possible. It is measured by greater control: understanding what a text says, expressing the intended message, noticing uncertainty, and knowing when further checking is worthwhile.','这并不意味着每句都要详细讲语法，也不意味着每条建议都必须质疑。投入程度应匹配任务：日常消息可快速检查，影响他人的提案则值得仔细修改。长期来看，可以找出重复问题并有针对性地练习，观察是否越来越少依赖同类帮助。成功不等于尽量少用助手，而是获得更强掌控力：读懂内容、表达原意、察觉不确定，并知道什么时候值得进一步核查。']
],[['independently','独立地','Try to understand the text independently.'],['intended meaning','原本想表达的意思','Keep the intended meaning.'],['optional style changes','可选的风格调整','Separate errors from optional style changes.'],['recurring problems','反复出现的问题','Look for recurring problems.'],['match the task','与任务相匹配','The amount of checking should match the task.']],[[
 '作者认为读前先翻译每一段的主要问题是什么？',['永远无法读完任何内容','缺少独立建立英文与含义联系的机会','一定会破坏原文文件'],1,'第二段关注学习机会，而不是断言翻译必然完全无效。'],
 ['AI 把简单消息改得过分正式，可能造成什么问题？',['自动提高所有读写能力','证明学习者已经掌握全部语法','文字虽漂亮，却不一定适合沟通目的'],2,'第三段以偏离 intended meaning 和目的说明润色不等于有效表达。'],
 ['文中用什么衡量成功？',['对理解、表达和不确定性的掌控增强','完全不再使用任何工具','每句都获得同样的语法长解释'],0,'结尾强调 greater control，而不是机械减少工具使用。']
 ],{task:'用英文描述你准备如何用 AI 学英语，并说明怎样检查自己是否真正掌握。',audience:'希望了解你学习计划的朋友',length:'200～280 词',requirements:['分别说明阅读与写作的练习流程','给出一个可观察的进步指标','说明你如何处理错误建议或改变原意的修改'],starter:'My immediate goal is…\nBefore asking for help, I will…\nI will know I am improving when…',model:'My immediate goal is to read software documentation and communicate clearly through written English. I plan to use AI for explanations and feedback, while keeping an independent attempt in each session.\n\nFor reading, I will choose a manageable passage and write down its main point before asking for help. I will then check whether I misunderstood a condition, a negative statement, or a reference to an earlier idea. After reading the explanation, I will return to the original passage. Progress should mean that similar passages require less help over time.\n\nFor writing, I will keep my first draft and ask the assistant to separate errors from optional style suggestions. I will check whether each revision preserves my meaning and whether it adds facts I did not provide. Then I will write a revised version myself. I will record recurring problems and practise them in new sentences. I do not expect every suggestion to be correct, and I will check important claims or unclear corrections before relying on them.\n\nEach week, I will try one familiar type of request without reading a model first. I will compare the completeness and accuracy of that attempt with an earlier draft. This should help me distinguish genuine independent improvement from the temporary benefit of copying a polished answer.'});
